/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=(), browsing-topics=()
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data:; connect-src 'self'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'

# The site loads no third-party script, style, font or image, so the policy above can be
# this strict. If you ever add one, you have a decision to make rather than a header to
# loosen.
#
# Two directives need explaining.
#
# script-src: the 'self' above is a PLACEHOLDER. Astro inlines small scripts into the
# HTML instead of emitting files, so 'self' alone blocks the theme toggle, the table's
# search and sort, and the calculator. `npm run build` runs scripts/csp-hashes.ts, which
# reads the real build output and rewrites this line in dist/_headers with a sha256 hash
# per inline script. Edit that script, not this line — anything written here by hand is
# overwritten at build time.
#
# style-src carries 'unsafe-inline' because the pages use inline style attributes (the
# table's per-row animation delay, for one) and CSP hashes cannot cover style attributes
# at all — only <style> elements. On a static site with no user-supplied content this is
# a small exposure, and it buys back the ability to keep script-src genuinely strict,
# which is where cross-site scripting actually lives.
